const socioCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · No dialect is "broken"', note: 'African American Vernacular English has systematic grammar (habitual "be": "she be working" = habitual aspect, distinct from "she working"). Linguistic findings: AAVE is rule-governed, expressive and legitimate — school failure comes from mismatch, not deficit.' },
    { math: '2 · Prestige is assigned, not inherent', note: '"Standard" English is the dialect of the powerful, standardised by print and institutions. RP/BBC accents, "proper" grammar — judgements of "correct" track class and power, not clarity (Lippi-Green\'s documentation of accent discrimination).' },
    { math: '3 · Variation is structured, not random', note: 'Labov\'s department store study (1966): r-pronunciation dropped with class and formality, in a smooth pattern. Sociolinguistics quantifies variation — variables correlate with class, age, gender, ethnicity in regular ways.' },
    { math: '4 · Language change from below', note: 'Change spreads through social networks: innovators are usually high-status young speakers, then the change spreads and becomes "standard". Marthas Vineyard (Labov, 1963) caught a change mid-flight.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/advanced/sociolinguistics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Sociolinguistics studies <strong>how language varies with society — class, region, gender,
    ethnicity, context</strong> — and finds the variation is structured, systematic and
    meaningful. Its sharpest finding: linguistic "correctness" is social authority, not internal
    quality, which makes the field quietly political.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dialect legitimacy, prestige, quantified variation, change from below" widget="equationStep" config={socioCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts with real-world consequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Code-switching:</strong> speakers alternate varieties by context — bi-dialectal competence, not confusion. Multilingual speakers do it sentence-internally, and it takes skill.</li>
    <li><strong>Register:</strong> the same speaker uses different varieties for job interview, pub and text message — every human is multilingual in registers.</li>
    <li><strong>Language death:</strong> ~half of the world's 7,000 languages are endangered — losing a language loses an ecology of knowledge (plant names, oral history, unique grammar). Revitalisation (Hebrew's revival is the success story; Māori and Welsh the ongoing ones) is applied sociolinguistics.</li>
    <li><strong>Language policy:</strong> official-language laws, mother-tongue education (children learn better in their first language — UNESCO's evidence), script choices. Linguistics advising governments, with stakes in millions of children's outcomes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Labov (1963, 1966); Lippi-Green, English with an Accent (1997); Crystal, Language Death (2000).</p>
</TopicPage>
